<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Qocho</h1><p class="st-meta">856 CE – 1335 CE (approx.) · capital Qocho</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Uyghur kingdom of the Turfan oasis where Buddhism, Manichaeism and Christianity flourished and whose rulers submitted early to Genghis Khan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate</a> fell in 840, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> groups moved south-west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> oases. By 856 they controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beshbaliq">Beshbaliq</a>, with Qocho (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gaochang-kingdom">Gaochang</a>) as a winter capital, establishing a kingdom under rulers with the title idiqut.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> adapted to oasis life, with irrigated fields, vineyards and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. The court first favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> became dominant, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bezeklik-caves">Bezeklik caves</a> were decorated with murals of Uyghur donors. The Uyghur script, derived from Sogdian, was used for a large literature of Buddhist and other texts, and Uyghur scribes later served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a>, and in 1209 the idiqut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barchuq-art-tegin">Barchuq Art Tegin</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> it remained a privileged vassal until rule passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> in the 14th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-qocho.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Qocho" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qocho_Uyghurs.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>856 CE – 1335 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qocho</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Barchuq Art Tegin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Manichaeism, Church of the East</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Uyghur</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gaochang Uyghur Kingdom, Idiqut state, Uyghur Kingdom of Qocho</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1096&amp;focus=kingdom-of-qocho" aria-label="Kingdom of Qocho on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6817 617 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Qocho lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7137 873L7400 770M7137 873L6820 915M7137 873L7559 780M7137 873L6937 875M7137 873L7020 950M7137 873L7103 937"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7400 770h0M6820 915h0M7559 780h0M6937 875h0M7020 950h0M7103 937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7137 873h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7137 873h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1096&amp;focus=kingdom-of-qocho">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
